  "platform": "Mega Drive",
  "summary": "Konami’s Mega Drive Contra gives four fighters distinct weapons and sends their campaign along branching routes.",
  "body": [
    "Contra: Hard Corps is Konami’s side-scrolling run-and-gun game, first released for Genesis in North America on 14 September 1994. A new task force confronts Colonel Bahamut after the theft of an alien cell intended for use in weapons. The player chooses from four distinct soldiers, each bringing a different selection of guns into the campaign. Two players can fight together with different characters. Running and jumping quickly lead into large enemy encounters, making the opening stages a succession of threats that demand attention to both movement and fire.",
    "The firing controls offer two useful relationships between aiming and movement. One lets the character move while shooting; the other holds the soldier in place while the player aims in different directions. Switching between them changes whether a dangerous pattern is best answered by retreating or by staying still to hit a particular target. Each character’s weapon set further changes that decision. A familiar enemy can therefore demand a different position when approached with another fighter, giving the choice at the start of the game lasting consequences.",
    "Decisions during the story can lead to different routes and endings, so clearing one path leaves other encounters to discover. Brief scenes connect the battles and present those choices without taking the action away from its side-on perspective. In Europe and Australia the game appeared as Probotector, with altered characters. The branching structure and four weapon sets make returning to the campaign more than a search for a cleaner performance along a single fixed route."
  ],
  "story_headings": [
    "A new task force",
    "Holding a position under fire",
    "The road divides"
  ],
